WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:02.320
Eighty per cent
of the British population

00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:05.280
say they visit a fish and chip shop
at least once a year,

00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:09.600
and we consume 382 million meals
from the chippie per annum.

00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:10.840
That’s pretty impressive

00:00:10.920 --> 00:00:14.560
for a combination that’s only
been with us for around 150 years –

00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:18.560
for the first fish and chip shop
only appeared in around 1860.

00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:21.760
Separately, both fried fish, and
chips, were enjoyed by the British

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:23.640
well before they came together.

00:00:23.720 --> 00:00:27.360
Chips as a term for something edible
was in use in the 18th Century,

00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:30.440
often in relation, rather curiously,
to oranges -

00:00:30.520 --> 00:00:33.000
orange chips
were candied chunks of peel.

00:00:33.080 --> 00:00:34.240
It was in the same century

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:37.920
that the potato was going from
knobbly curiosity to staple food,

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:39.400
especially for the poor,

00:00:39.480 --> 00:00:42.760
and given how well potatoes
lend themselves to deep frying,

00:00:42.840 --> 00:00:46.400
it was inescapable that potato chips
would soon be on the scene.

00:00:46.840 --> 00:00:49.720
By the Victorian era,
chipped potatoes were everywhere,

00:00:49.960 --> 00:00:52.080
from the delicate little
‘straw potatoes’ -

00:00:52.160 --> 00:00:54.840
which resembled, well, straw -
eaten by the rich,

00:00:54.920 --> 00:00:57.760
to the French street food,
which Dickens described as

00:00:57.840 --> 00:01:01.480
“husky chips of potatoes fried
with some reluctant drops of oil”.

00:01:01.560 --> 00:01:05.240
The British preferred baked potatoes,
but they ate them fried too.

00:01:05.320 --> 00:01:08.880
Fried fish, meanwhile, was also on
the tables of the rich and the poor.

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:09.960
Bread-crumbed filets,

00:01:10.040 --> 00:01:12.720
delicately fried in butter
and garnished with fried parsley,

00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:14.960
were a staple for upmarket meals.

00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:18.240
Whitebait, fried in lard, were
considered a delicious delicacy.

00:01:18.640 --> 00:01:20.840
But there was another place
fried fish could be bought,

00:01:20.920 --> 00:01:22.440
and that was the street -

00:01:22.520 --> 00:01:25.080
in the Jewish quarters
of London and other big cities.

00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:27.920
There, Sephardic Jews
sold cold fried fish

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:29.400
intended for eating on the Sabbath

00:01:29.480 --> 00:01:31.760
when no cooking was allowed
in Jewish homes.

00:01:31.960 --> 00:01:35.160
It quickly took off,
being cheap, filling and tasty,

00:01:35.480 --> 00:01:38.400
and fried fish shops and
market stalls - hot and cold -

00:01:38.480 --> 00:01:40.440
sprung up in cities across the UK.

00:01:40.520 --> 00:01:42.520
It was inevitable
that these two street foods,

00:01:42.600 --> 00:01:45.520
so popular with the masses, would
end up being sold in combination.

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:50.240
It proved a winning formula and fish
and chip shops took off immediately.

00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:52.680
Trawl fishing and the
railway boom of the 1870s

00:01:52.760 --> 00:01:56.200
helped fish to reach inland
areas quicker than ever before,

00:01:56.280 --> 00:01:58.520
and potatoes were already
grown everywhere.

00:01:58.760 --> 00:02:02.800
By 1910 there were 25,000 fish and
chip shops in the UK,

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:05.720
and by 1929 there were 10,000 more.

00:02:06.040 --> 00:02:09.160
Such was the hold of fish and chips -
and its genuine goodness,

00:02:09.240 --> 00:02:11.800
especially for its mainly
working-class consumers -

00:02:11.880 --> 00:02:13.160
that during the Second World War,

00:02:13.240 --> 00:02:15.400
fish and chips
remained off the ration,

00:02:15.480 --> 00:02:16.680
though the type of fish available

00:02:16.760 --> 00:02:19.840
was not always the most delicious
or desirable of species.

00:02:20.320 --> 00:02:23.720
Across the UK, accompaniments vary -
from the mushy peas of the north,

00:02:23.800 --> 00:02:26.680
to curry sauce, ketchup, mayonnaise
or the chip shop spice

00:02:26.760 --> 00:02:28.800
which is on every counter in Hull.

00:02:28.880 --> 00:02:30.760
Salt and vinegar remain the staples.

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:32.440
In the 21st Century chippie,

00:02:32.520 --> 00:02:36.000
vegetable fats have often replaced
beef dripping or lard for frying.

00:02:36.280 --> 00:02:39.040
Newspaper used to be
the wrapping of choice - outlawed,

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:43.360
unless fresh from the printers and
unsullied by readers’ hands, in 1968.

00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:46.480
Now it’s mainly plain paper
and polystyrene,

00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:49.920
but the skillful wrapping and
excitement of unwrapping remains.

00:02:50.440 --> 00:02:52.680
There are, however, fish and chips…

00:02:52.760 --> 00:02:54.200
and fish and chips.

00:02:54.280 --> 00:02:56.600
Witness the disappointment
on the faces of tourists,

00:02:56.680 --> 00:02:59.520
lured by the promise
of a great British delicacy,

00:02:59.600 --> 00:03:02.280
only to find they’ve happened
upon the bad version,

00:03:02.360 --> 00:03:04.480
all grease and soggy batter.

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:05.960
But then when it’s good…

00:03:06.040 --> 00:03:10.240
well, it’s crispy and fluffy, with a
hit of vinegar, and a punch of salt.

00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:11.600
It's unbeatable.

